// The `verdict` format, v0. This file is NORMATIVE: the Rust parser in
// diet/src/formats/verdict.rs implements it and is not itself the definition.
//
// A verdict is the whole answer of a constrained fork asked whether a
// nominated pair is a supersession: the collector found an entry recorded
// at turn 5 and prose at turn 18 that names its anchors, and one fork is
// spent asking what the prose does to the entry. The model judges; this
// grammar formats; the reconciler applies. What it applies is a link, never
// an overwrite -- but only if the answer is one of four words, because the
// reconciler branches on the word and a fifth outcome would be a branch
// nobody wrote.
//
// So the answer is one of DONE, PARTIAL, NOT_THIS, SUPERSEDED -- whole,
// first, and alone. The rules, each of which closes a hole a tolerant reader
// would have opened:
//
//   1. THE VERDICT COMES FIRST. `I think it is DONE` is prose containing a
//      verdict, and a reader that searched for the word would find one in
//      `not DONE yet` too, with the opposite meaning.
//   2. THE VERDICT IS A WHOLE WORD. `DONEISH` and `PARTIALLY` are not
//      verdicts. What refuses them is the anchored document: after the
//      verdict comes a reason or nothing, and `ISH` is neither. A boundary
//      check on the verdict token itself would say the same thing a second
//      time and could not be made to fail on its own, so the rule lives in
//      one place and the anchor is what a seeded fault removes. The
//      boundary check that does carry its own weight is in `upper_verdict`
//      below, where a reason may name `DONE_MARKER` without that naming
//      being a second answer.
//   3. THERE IS ONE VERDICT. A reason may follow, set off by a colon, a
//      dash or parentheses, and the reason may not contain a second verdict
//      written as one -- upper case, a whole token: `DONE - or PARTIAL` is
//      two answers, and the reconciler cannot apply two. A lower-case
//      `done` inside a reason is English.
//   4. ONE LINE. A second line is a second answer, or a discussion; the
//      fork was not asked for either.
//
// Case is not significant, and `NOT THIS` with a space is `NOT_THIS`: a
// register difference is not a different judgment. A bare reason with no
// verdict is not a verdict. An empty answer is not a verdict either; the
// caller records a silence, which is a typed outcome of its own.

sp = _{ " " | "\t" }
ws = _{ " " | "\t" | "\r" | "\n" }
nl = _{ "\r\n" | "\n" }

// Anchored at both ends: a verdict is a property of the whole answer.
document = { SOI ~ ws* ~ verdict ~ reason? ~ ws* ~ EOI }

// No spelling here is a prefix of another, and the document's end anchor is
// what keeps a longer word that merely begins with one from being a verdict
// (rule 2).
verdict = @{ ^"SUPERSEDED" | ^"NOT_THIS" | ^"NOT THIS" | ^"PARTIAL" | ^"DONE" }
word_char = _{ ASCII_ALPHANUMERIC | "_" }

// A reason: set off by a colon that may hug the verdict, by a dash with
// whitespace on both sides, or bracketed. One line, and no second verdict
// in it (rule 3). A verdict inside a reason is one written in the answer's
// register -- upper case, a whole token -- and `it is done; nothing more`
// is English, not a second answer. Preserved verbatim by the parser, typed
// as a reason.
reason = { paren_reason | setoff_reason }
paren_reason = { sp* ~ "(" ~ sp* ~ paren_text ~ ")" }
paren_text = @{ paren_token ~ (sp+ ~ paren_token)* }
paren_token = _{ !upper_verdict ~ (!sp ~ !nl ~ !")" ~ ANY)+ }
setoff_reason = { (colon_setoff | dash_setoff) ~ reason_text }
colon_setoff = _{ sp* ~ ":" ~ sp* }
dash_setoff = _{ sp+ ~ ("—" | "–" | "--" | "-") ~ sp+ }
reason_text = @{ reason_token ~ (sp+ ~ reason_token)* }
reason_token = _{ !upper_verdict ~ (!sp ~ !nl ~ ANY)+ }

// A verdict written as an answer would write it: upper case, and whole --
// `DONE_MARKER` in a reason is an identifier the reason names, not a second
// answer, and the boundary check is what tells them apart.
upper_verdict = _{ ("SUPERSEDED" | "NOT_THIS" | "NOT THIS" | "PARTIAL" | "DONE") ~ !word_char }
